      "text": "Let the two numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). According to the problem, we have the following equations:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe recognize that the expression \\( x^2 - y^2 \\) can be factored using the difference of squares formula:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\n\\]\nSubstituting the given sum \\( x + y = 6 \\) into the factorized form, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = 6(x - y)\n\\]\nWe know from the second equation that \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\), so we can equate the two expressions:\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 6, we find:\n\\[\nx - y = 2\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 6 \\)\n2. \\( x - y = 2 \\)\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations together:\n\\[\n(x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n2x = 8\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 2, we find:\n\\[\nx = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = 4 \\) back into the first equation:\n\\[\n4 + y = 6\n\\]\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we find:\n\\[\ny = 2\n\\]\n\nThe two numbers are \\( x = 4 \\) and \\( y = 2 \\). The positive difference of these two numbers is:\n\\[\nx - y = 4 - 2 = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
