      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers as \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). According to the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe can use the identity for the difference of squares, which states:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given sum \\( x + y = 6 \\) into the identity, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = 6(x - y)\n\\]\n\nWe know from the second piece of information that \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\), so we can substitute 12 for \\( x^2 - y^2 \\):\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x - y \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 6:\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n\\]\n\nThe positive difference of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can find \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) using \\( x + y = 6 \\) and \\( x - y = 2 \\). Adding these two equations, we get:\n\\[\n(x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2 \\implies 2x = 8 \\implies x = 4\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get:\n\\[\n(x + y) - (x - y) = 6 - 2 \\implies 2y = 4 \\implies y = 2\n\\]\n\nSo, the two numbers are \\( x = 4 \\) and \\( y = 2 \\). The positive difference is:\n\\[\n|4 - 2| = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is indeed \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
