      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers by \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). According to the problem, we have two pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe can use the identity for the difference of squares, which states that \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\). Substituting the value of \\( x + y \\) from the first equation, we get:\n   \\[\n   (x + y)(x - y) = 12\n   \\]\nSince \\( x + y = 6 \\), we can substitute this into the equation:\n   \\[\n   6(x - y) = 12\n   \\]\nTo find \\( x - y \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 6:\n   \\[\n   x - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n   \\]\n\nThus, the positive difference of the two numbers is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2}\n   \\]",
